3	//! Boot-time reconciliation of the starter Cedar policy set + the eight system roles
4	//! (SMA-444 Task 17; converge-to-code since SMA-477). Runs once per `AppState::new`, AFTER the
5	//! policy store is constructed but BEFORE the initial `PolicySnapshot` compiles — so a fresh
6	//! database is seeded before the first request is decided.
7	//!
8	//! **These rows are code-owned.** Boot converges each starter policy and each system role row
9	//! to the code-defined content; the database was the one place that ownership was not enforced.
10	//! What used to be a compare-and-WARN that never wrote — and therefore warned forever, since a
11	//! generated policy source changes with every new write action — is now:
12	//!
13	//! - a routine code change converges silently (INFO);
14	//! - a row written by a NEWER release is left alone (`StaleBinary`) — see
15	//!   `authz::roles::STARTER_POLICY_REVISION` for why that matters with one shared table. When
16	//!   such a row's provenance does NOT check out, the deferral still stands (an older binary must
17	//!   never rewrite a newer release's row) but it is reported as the divergence it is: WARN, and
18	//!   the `externally_modified` metric label rather than `stale_binary`;
19	//! - an out-of-band edit converges LOUDLY: WARN, a metric, and one audit entry capturing what
20	//!   was overwritten, since converging destroys the evidence.
21	//!
22	//! **Failure posture (SMA-477 D12), applied symmetrically to BOTH halves.** Converging an
23	//! existing row is best-effort: an error is logged, counted, and skipped, because that row
24	//! governed decisions perfectly well before this change and refusing to boot would turn a
25	//! transient database blip into an outage. SEEDING an absent row stays fatal:
26	//!
27	//! - for a policy, `AppState::new` documents that the initial snapshot always compiles at least
28	//!   the starter set, and a replica that booted with a partial policy set would deny everything;
29	//! - for a role, `role_grant.role_key` carries an FK to `role.key` (`fk_role_grant_role`), so a
30	//!   replica that booted past a failed `platform_admin` INSERT does not fail at boot — it fails
31	//!   the first bootstrap-admin grant with a raw foreign-key violation, at authentication time.
32	//!
33	//! Both halves therefore snapshot the persisted ids ONCE before their loop and decide fatality
34	//! against it ([`classify_failure`]).
35	
36	use metrics::counter;
37	use paigasus_iam_core::authz::model::root_prn;
38	use paigasus_iam_core::authz::reconcile::{RoleOutcome, StarterPolicyOutcome, policy_kind_str};
39	use paigasus_iam_core::authz::roles::{self as authz_roles, STARTER_POLICY_IDS, STARTER_POLICY_REVISION};
40	use paigasus_iam_core::{AuditEntry, AuditLog, AuditOutcome, AuthzError, Clock, IdGenerator, PolicyDocument, SystemPolicyReconciler, SystemRoleReconciler};
41	use paigasus_observability::names;
42	use std::sync::Arc;
